/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/cwna.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("cwna", [["cw1",0,1,"An engineer reads that an access point is transmitting at 100 mW. What is this power level in dBm?",["10 dBm","20 dBm","30 dBm","100 dBm"],1,"10 × log10(100) = 20, so 100 mW is 20 dBm. 10 dBm would be 10 mW and 30 dBm would be 1,000 mW.","Domain 1 – RF math",["10 dBm is 10 mW; 100 mW is ten times more power, which adds another 10 dB.",null,"30 dBm is 1,000 mW (1 W), ten times more than 100 mW.","100 dBm confuses the mW number with dBm; it would be 10^10 mW, far beyond any Wi-Fi radio."],null],["cw2",0,1,"A radio outputs 17 dBm through a cable with 2 dB of loss to an antenna rated at 6 dBi. What is the EIRP?",["23 dBm","25 dBm","19 dBm","21 dBm"],3,"EIRP = 17 − 2 + 6 = 21 dBm. Adding the cable loss instead of subtracting it gives the tempting 25 dBm.","Domain 1 – EIRP and link budget",["23 dBm ignores the 2 dB of cable loss and simply adds output power and antenna gain.","25 dBm adds the 2 dB cable loss instead of subtracting it; losses always reduce the power reaching the antenna.","19 dBm subtracts the cable loss but forgets to add the 6 dBi antenna gain, which increases the radiated power.",null],null],["cw3",0,1,"A laptop reports a received signal of −65 dBm and a noise floor of −92 dBm. What is the SNR?",["27 dB","157 dB","−27 dB","92 dB"],0,"SNR is signal minus noise: −65 − (−92) = 27 dB. Adding the absolute values gives the wrong 157.","Domain 1 – Signal metrics",[null,"157 dB adds the absolute values of signal and noise; SNR is the difference between them, not the sum.","A negative SNR would mean noise is stronger than signal; here the signal at -65 dBm is 27 dB above the -92 dBm noise floor.","92 dB is just the noise floor value without its sign; SNR must compare the signal against that noise."],null],["cw4",0,1,"A 40 mW transmitter has 3 dB of gain added to its output. About how much power results?",["120 mW","43 mW","80 mW","400 mW"],2,"Every 3 dB of gain roughly doubles power, so 40 mW becomes about 80 mW. 10 dB of gain would give 400 mW.","Domain 1 – RF math",["120 mW would be tripling the power; 3 dB of gain roughly doubles power rather than adding a factor of three.","43 mW adds the 3 dB value directly to the milliwatt figure; decibels are ratios, not milliwatts to add.",null,"400 mW is a tenfold increase, which is what 10 dB of gain gives, not 3 dB."],null],["cw5",0,1,"Which statement about 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz signals is correct?",["Both bands share one wavelength","2.4 GHz has the shorter wavelength","5 GHz has the shorter wavelength","Wavelength depends on transmit power"],2,"Wavelength is inversely related to frequency, so the higher 5 GHz band has the shorter wavelength (about 6 cm vs 12.5 cm). Power changes amplitude, not wavelength.","Domain 1 – RF characteristics",["Wavelength depends on frequency, so two bands at different frequencies cannot share one wavelength.","It is the reverse: 2.4 GHz is the lower frequency, so it has the longer wavelength (about 12.5 cm).",null,"Transmit power changes the amplitude of the wave, not its wavelength, which is fixed by frequency."],null],["cw6",0,1,"In a warehouse full of metal racking, a client receives several delayed copies of the same transmission. Which RF behavior mainly causes this?",["Reflection","Absorption","Refraction","Free space path loss"],0,"Metal surfaces reflect RF, creating multiple paths that arrive at different times (multipath). Absorption weakens a signal but does not create copies.","Domain 1 – RF behaviors",[null,"Absorption converts RF energy into heat and weakens the signal, but it does not produce delayed copies of it.","Refraction bends a wave as it passes between media of different density; it does not explain many echoes off metal racks.","Free space path loss is the weakening from the wave spreading out over distance and does not create multiple copies."],null],["cw7",0,1,"Signal in a lecture hall is strong when it is empty but drops sharply once it fills with students. Which behavior explains this?",["Scattering","Diffraction","Reflection","Absorption"],3,"The human body is mostly water, which absorbs RF energy and converts it to heat. Diffraction is bending around edges, not loss inside a material.","Domain 1 – RF behaviors",["Scattering happens off rough or uneven surfaces and small objects; it does not explain the energy lost into a crowd of water-filled bodies.","Diffraction is bending around the edge of an obstacle; the loss here happens inside the people themselves.","Reflection bounces signals off smooth surfaces such as metal or glass; human bodies mostly absorb 2.4 and 5 GHz energy rather than reflect it.",null],null],["cw8",0,1,"Users just behind the corner of a thick concrete wall still get a weak signal even though the wall blocks the direct path. Which behavior lets the signal reach them?",["Absorption in the wall","Diffraction around the wall's edge","Refraction through the air","Polarization of the signal"],1,"Diffraction bends a wave around the edge of an obstacle, leaving a weaker RF shadow behind it. Absorption only removes energy.","Domain 1 – RF behaviors",["Absorption only removes energy inside the wall; it cannot redirect the signal around the corner to the users.",null,"Refraction is bending as a wave passes through media of different density, such as air layers or glass, not bending around a wall edge.","Polarization is the orientation of the electric field; it does not let a signal travel around an obstruction."],null],["cw9",0,1,"An outdoor bridge with a completely clear path still shows lower received signal as the distance is increased. What is the main reason?",["The cable VSWR rises with distance","Water vapor absorbs most 5 GHz energy","Multipath always cancels the main signal","The wavefront spreads out with distance"],3,"Free space path loss comes from the wave spreading over a larger area as it travels, even with no obstacles. Weather effects are minor at these distances and VSWR does not depend on link length.","Domain 1 – RF behaviors",["VSWR reflects impedance mismatch in the cable and connectors, which does not change as the link gets longer.","Water vapor causes only minor attenuation at 5 GHz over typical bridge distances; it is not the main cause of this loss.","Multipath can cause fading, but it does not always cancel the signal and a clear outdoor path has little of it; it does not explain a steady drop with distance.",null],null],["cw10",0,1,"A point-to-point link was installed with one Yagi mounted vertically and the other horizontally. What is the likely result?",["Higher gain from polarization diversity","Heavy loss from polarization mismatch","No effect, since Yagis are omnidirectional","A wider beamwidth on both antennas"],1,"Antennas on a link should share the same polarization; a 90-degree mismatch causes large signal loss. Yagis are semi-directional, not omnidirectional.","Domain 1 – Antennas",["Polarization diversity is a deliberate multi-antenna design; mismatching the two ends of a link loses signal rather than adding gain.",null,"Yagis are semi-directional antennas, so the premise is wrong and polarization still matters.","Rotating an antenna changes its polarization, not its beamwidth; the beam pattern is unchanged but the link suffers mismatch loss."],null],["cw11",0,1,"Two buildings can see each other's rooftop antennas, but trees intrude on the area just below the visual path and the link is unstable. What should the engineer address?",["The ground plane under the mast","The antennas' back lobe","Clearance of the Fresnel zone","The horizontal beamwidth only"],2,"RF line of sight needs most of the first Fresnel zone clear, not just a visual path. Obstructions in it reduce and destabilize the signal.","Domain 1 – Point-to-point links",["A ground plane affects some antenna types' patterns, but the problem here is trees intruding into the path near the line of sight.","The back lobe is energy radiated behind the antenna; it does not explain an obstruction between the two buildings.",null,"Horizontal beamwidth describes antenna coverage width; the trees are blocking the Fresnel zone, which beamwidth changes will not fix."],null],["cw12",0,1,"A company needs a high-gain, narrow-beam link between two sites several kilometers apart. Which antenna type fits best?",["Parabolic dish","Omnidirectional dipole","Indoor ceiling patch","Wide-angle sector"],0,"Highly directional parabolic dishes provide the most gain and narrowest beam for long point-to-point links. Omnis and sectors spread energy over wide areas.","Domain 1 – Antennas",[null,"An omnidirectional dipole spreads energy all around with low gain, which is poor for a multi-kilometer point-to-point link.","An indoor ceiling patch is meant for indoor coverage and has modest gain; it is not built for long outdoor links.","A sector antenna covers a wide arc for point-to-multipoint service, so its gain and beam are too broad for a long single link."],null],["cw13",0,1,"An antenna datasheet lists a gain of 5 dBd. What is the equivalent gain in dBi?",["2.86 dBi","7.14 dBi","5 dBi","10 dBi"],1,"A dipole has 2.14 dBi of gain, so dBi = dBd + 2.14 = 7.14 dBi. Subtracting instead gives 2.86.","Domain 1 – RF math",["2.86 dBi subtracts the 2.14 dB dipole gain; converting dBd to dBi means adding it because dBi uses a lower reference.",null,"5 dBi would treat dBd and dBi as the same unit, ignoring the 2.14 dB gain of the dipole reference.","10 dBi doubles the number instead of adding the fixed 2.14 dB offset between the two references."],null],["cw14",0,1,"An AP radio is described as 3x3:2. What does this mean?",["3 spatial streams and 2 receive chains","3 streams, 3 antennas, 2 bands","3 radios per band and 2 bands in total","3 transmit chains, 3 receive chains, 2 streams"],3,"MIMO notation is transmit chains × receive chains : spatial streams. The radio can send two streams while using all three chains for diversity.","Domain 1 – MIMO",["MIMO notation lists transmit chains first, then receive chains, then streams, so 3x3:2 has two spatial streams, not three.","The notation says nothing about bands or antenna count; the number after the colon is spatial streams.","3x3:2 describes radio chains and streams in one radio, not the number of radios or bands.",null],null],["cw15",0,2,"Which organization writes and publishes the 802.11 standard and its amendments?",["IEEE","Wi-Fi Alliance","IETF","FCC"],0,"The IEEE maintains 802.11. The Wi-Fi Alliance certifies interoperability, the IETF writes internet protocols such as RADIUS, and the FCC regulates spectrum in the US.","Domain 2 – Standards bodies",[null,"The Wi-Fi Alliance certifies interoperability and names generations, but it does not write the 802.11 standard.","The IETF writes internet protocols such as RADIUS and IP, not the 802.11 wireless LAN standard.","The FCC regulates spectrum use in the United States; it does not author technical standards like 802.11."],null],["cw16",0,2,"A buyer wants assurance that a new client adapter will work with WPA3 on other vendors' access points. What should they look for?",["FCC equipment authorization for the adapter","IEEE 802.11 ratification of the adapter","Wi-Fi CERTIFIED WPA3 from the Wi-Fi Alliance","An IETF RFC number on the product box"],2,"The Wi-Fi Alliance tests products for interoperability and security features such as WPA3. FCC authorization covers emissions, not interoperability.","Domain 2 – Standards bodies",["FCC equipment authorization covers emissions and legal operation, not whether WPA3 interoperates with other vendors.","The IEEE ratifies standards, not individual products, and it does not test interoperability.",null,"IETF RFCs define internet protocols; they are not product certifications and say nothing about WPA3 interoperability."],null],["cw17",0,2,"Who decides which channels and maximum transmit power an AP may legally use in a given country?",["The Wi-Fi Alliance","The IEEE 802.11 working group","That country's regulatory authority","The AP manufacturer"],2,"National regulators, such as the FCC in the United States, set legal frequencies and power limits. The IEEE defines how the protocol works within those rules.","Domain 2 – Regulations",["The Wi-Fi Alliance certifies products but has no legal authority over channels or power in any country.","The IEEE 802.11 working group defines how the protocol works, but each country's regulator decides what is legal to use.",null,"The manufacturer must build products to meet local rules, but it does not set those rules."],null],["cw18",0,2,"An engineer is planning 20 MHz 2.4 GHz channels for an office in North America. Which set avoids overlap?",["1, 6 and 11","1, 5 and 9","2, 7 and 12","1, 4, 8 and 11"],0,"2.4 GHz channels are 5 MHz apart but about 20 MHz wide, so 1, 6 and 11 are the standard non-overlapping set in North America.","Domain 2 – Channels",[null,"Channels 1, 5 and 9 are only 20 MHz apart in center frequency, so their roughly 22 MHz-wide signals still overlap slightly and it is not the standard plan.","2, 7 and 12 has the right spacing, but channel 12 is not usable at normal power in North America, so it is not the standard plan there.","Four channels at 1, 4, 8 and 11 are only three channels apart, so adjacent ones overlap heavily."],null],["cw19",0,2,"Which 802.11 amendment introduced OFDMA and BSS coloring?",["802.11g","802.11ac","802.11n","802.11ax"],3,"802.11ax (HE, Wi-Fi 6) added OFDMA, BSS coloring, Target Wake Time and 1024-QAM. 802.11ac introduced downlink MU-MIMO but not OFDMA.","Domain 2 – 802.11ax",["802.11g brought OFDM to 2.4 GHz at up to 54 Mbps; it has no OFDMA or BSS coloring.","802.11ac added wider channels, 256-QAM and downlink MU-MIMO, but OFDMA and BSS coloring came later with 802.11ax.","802.11n introduced MIMO and 40 MHz channels, not OFDMA or BSS coloring.",null],null],["cw20",0,2,"A client's datasheet lists only VHT (802.11ac) rates. In which band can it use those rates?",["2.4 GHz only","5 GHz only","2.4 and 5 GHz","6 GHz only"],1,"802.11ac VHT is defined for 5 GHz only. 802.11ax (HE) works in 2.4, 5 and 6 GHz, which is the tempting mix-up.","Domain 2 – PHYs",["VHT is not defined for 2.4 GHz; a 2.4 GHz-only device would use HT or older rates.",null,"802.11ac VHT rates are 5 GHz only; in 2.4 GHz the client falls back to HT (802.11n) or older rates.","6 GHz Wi-Fi uses HE (802.11ax) or newer; VHT clients cannot operate in 6 GHz at all."],null],["cw21",0,2,"Which amendment first introduced MIMO and 40 MHz channels under the name HT?",["802.11ac","802.11a","802.11g","802.11n"],3,"802.11n (High Throughput, Wi-Fi 4) added MIMO and 40 MHz channels. 802.11ac later added 80 and 160 MHz.","Domain 2 – PHYs",["802.11ac (VHT) extended MIMO with 80/160 MHz channels and MU-MIMO; it did not introduce HT.","802.11a introduced OFDM in 5 GHz with a single stream and 20 MHz channels, with no MIMO.","802.11g brought ERP-OFDM to 2.4 GHz with single-stream 20 MHz channels, not MIMO.",null],null],["cw22",0,2,"An old barcode scanner supports only 1, 2, 5.5 and 11 Mbps. Which PHY does it use?",["ERP-OFDM (802.11g)","HR/DSSS (802.11b)","OFDM (802.11a)","HT (802.11n)"],1,"Those four rates are the DSSS and HR/DSSS rates of 802.11b. ERP-OFDM and OFDM rates start at 6 Mbps.","Domain 2 – PHYs",["ERP-OFDM (802.11g) uses OFDM rates from 6 to 54 Mbps; 1-11 Mbps only would indicate DSSS/HR-DSSS.",null,"802.11a OFDM runs at 6-54 Mbps in 5 GHz and never uses 1, 2, 5.5 or 11 Mbps.","HT (802.11n) uses MCS-based rates starting at 6.5 Mbps; a device limited to 1-11 Mbps is 802.11b."],null],["cw23",0,2,"An admin wants to add a 6 GHz radio to an existing WPA2-Personal SSID. What is the problem?",["6 GHz needs 802.11b protection","6 GHz allows only 20 MHz channels","6 GHz needs WPA3 or Enhanced Open","6 GHz cannot use any passphrase"],2,"Wi-Fi 6E rules require WPA3 (SAE or Enterprise) or OWE with PMF in 6 GHz; WPA2 is not allowed. 6 GHz supports wide channels and WPA3-Personal uses a passphrase.","Domain 2 – 6 GHz",["802.11b does not exist in 6 GHz and no protection mechanism for it is needed there.","6 GHz supports 20, 40, 80 and 160 MHz (and 320 MHz with Wi-Fi 7), so channel width is not the issue.",null,"WPA3-Personal (SAE) still uses a passphrase in 6 GHz; only WPA2-Personal's PSK method is disallowed."],null],["cw24",0,2,"An AP on 5 GHz channel 100 suddenly changes channel and clients disconnect briefly. The log shows a radar event. Which requirement caused this?",["Dynamic Frequency Selection","Transmit Power Control","Clear Channel Assessment","Automatic rate selection"],0,"DFS requires devices in radar-shared channels to leave when radar is detected. TPC controls power levels, not channel changes after radar.","Domain 2 – DFS and TPC",[null,"TPC lowers transmit power to reduce interference; it does not force a channel change after radar detection.","CCA is how a station senses whether the medium is busy before transmitting; it is not a regulatory radar requirement.","Rate selection changes data rates based on signal quality, not the channel after a radar event."],null],["cw25",0,2,"Why do clients at the edge of an 802.11ax cell never reach 1024-QAM data rates?",["1024-QAM works only on 2.4 GHz","1024-QAM needs a very high SNR near the AP","1024-QAM is limited to legacy clients","1024-QAM is disabled whenever DFS is on"],1,"Denser modulation packs more bits per symbol but needs a clean, strong signal. Far from the AP, SNR drops and the rate shifts to simpler modulation.","Domain 2 – 802.11ax",["1024-QAM is part of 802.11ax and can be used in 2.4, 5 and 6 GHz, so band is not the reason.",null,"Legacy clients cannot use 1024-QAM at all; it requires 802.11ax (HE) capable devices.","DFS governs radar detection on certain 5 GHz channels and does not disable any modulation type."],null],["cw26",0,2,"A dense office wants 40 MHz channels on 2.4 GHz for more speed. What is the best advice?",["Avoid it: 40 MHz exists only in 6 GHz","Use it: 2.4 GHz has four clean 40 MHz channels","Use it: bonding removes co-channel contention","Avoid it: only one clean 40 MHz channel fits"],3,"The 2.4 GHz band is too narrow for more than one non-overlapping 40 MHz channel, so bonding there causes heavy overlap. 40 MHz is common in 5 GHz.","Domain 2 – Channels",["40 MHz channels were introduced by 802.11n and are widely used in 5 GHz; they are not limited to 6 GHz.","The 2.4 GHz band is only about 83.5 MHz wide, so it cannot hold four clean 40 MHz channels.","Channel bonding makes each channel wider and leaves fewer non-overlapping channels, which increases co-channel contention.",null],null],["cw27",0,2,"Which amendment defines fast BSS transition so 802.1X clients can roam without a full reauthentication?",["802.11r","802.11k","802.11v","802.11w"],0,"802.11r (FT) pre-derives keys for the target AP. 802.11k gives neighbor reports and 802.11w protects management frames.","Domain 2 – Amendments",[null,"802.11k provides radio measurements and neighbor reports to help pick a target AP, but it does not speed up the key exchange.","802.11v provides BSS transition management and other network hints; it does not define fast key derivation for roaming.","802.11w protects management frames such as deauthentication; it has nothing to do with fast roaming."],null],["cw28",0,2,"A client asks its AP for a list of nearby APs to speed up roaming. Which amendment provides this neighbor report?",["802.11w","802.11r","802.11k","802.11e"],2,"802.11k radio resource measurement includes neighbor reports. 802.11r speeds up the key exchange once a target is chosen.","Domain 2 – Amendments",["802.11w defines protected management frames, not neighbor reports.","802.11r speeds up the authentication and key exchange during a roam, but it does not provide the list of nearby APs.",null,"802.11e defines QoS (EDCA), which prioritizes traffic classes rather than reporting neighboring APs."],null],["cw29",0,2,"A spec sheet says the AP supports Wi-Fi 5. Which amendment is that?",["802.11n","802.11ax","802.11ac","802.11be"],2,"The Wi-Fi Alliance names are Wi-Fi 4 (n), Wi-Fi 5 (ac), Wi-Fi 6/6E (ax) and Wi-Fi 7 (be).","Domain 2 – Generation names",["802.11n is Wi-Fi 4 under the Wi-Fi Alliance generation names.","802.11ax is Wi-Fi 6 (and Wi-Fi 6E in the 6 GHz band).",null,"802.11be is Wi-Fi 7, two generations after Wi-Fi 5."],null],["cw30",0,2,"Which list shows adjacent, non-overlapping 20 MHz channels in the 5 GHz U-NII-1 band?",["36, 40, 44, 48","1, 6, 11, 14","36, 38, 40, 42","100, 101, 102, 103"],0,"5 GHz channel numbers are 5 MHz apart, so 20 MHz channels step by four: 36, 40, 44 and 48 in U-NII-1.","Domain 2 – Channels",[null,"1, 6, 11 and 14 are 2.4 GHz channels, and 14 is only allowed in Japan for 802.11b; none are in U-NII-1.","Channels 38 and 42 are the center numbers used for 40 and 80 MHz bonded channels, so this list mixes widths and overlaps.","5 GHz channel numbers are 5 MHz apart, so 100, 101, 102 and 103 would overlap almost entirely and are not valid 20 MHz channels."],null],["cw31",0,2,"What does OFDMA let an 802.11ax AP do?",["Transmit on 2.4 GHz channel 14 in all regions","Bond two separate bands into one wide channel","Encrypt frames without any 4-way handshake","Serve several clients at once using resource units"],3,"OFDMA divides a channel into resource units so several clients can be served in one transmission, cutting overhead for small frames.","Domain 2 – 802.11ax",["Channel 14 is permitted only in Japan and only for 802.11b; OFDMA does not change regional channel rules.","Combining separate bands is not OFDMA; it relates to multi-link operation in Wi-Fi 7, while OFDMA subdivides one channel.","OFDMA is a multiple-access method, not encryption; frames are still protected with keys from the 4-way handshake.",null],null],["cw32",0,2,"Battery-powered IoT sensors on an 802.11ax network need longer battery life. Which feature lets the AP schedule when they wake?",["BSS coloring","Target Wake Time","MU-MIMO","Transmit beamforming"],1,"TWT lets the AP and client agree wake schedules so the radio can sleep longer. BSS coloring helps with spatial reuse, not power saving.","Domain 2 – 802.11ax",["BSS coloring tags frames from each BSS to improve spatial reuse in overlapping cells; it does not schedule client wake times.",null,"MU-MIMO sends separate spatial streams to several clients at once to improve capacity, not battery life.","Transmit beamforming focuses energy toward a client to improve signal; it does not let a client sleep longer."],null],["cw33",0,3,"A capture shows beacon frames every 102.4 ms. What 802.11 frame type is a beacon?",["Null function","Control","Data","Management"],3,"Beacons, probes, authentication and association frames are management frames. Null function is a data subtype.","Domain 3 – Frame types",["Null function is a data frame subtype with no payload, often used to signal power-save state; beacons are not null frames.","Control frames such as ACK, RTS and CTS help deliver other frames; beacons announce the BSS and are management frames.","Data frames carry user payload (or null data); a beacon carries BSS information, not user data.",null],null],["cw34",0,3,"Which 802.11 frame type carries ACK, RTS and CTS frames?",["Management","Control","Data","Action"],1,"ACK, RTS, CTS and Block Ack are control frames that help deliver other frames. Action frames are a management subtype.","Domain 3 – Frame types",["Management frames include beacons, probes, authentication and association, not ACK, RTS or CTS.",null,"Data frames carry payload; ACK, RTS and CTS carry no user data and assist delivery.","Action frames are a management frame subtype used for things like Block Ack setup and 802.11k/v requests, not a separate frame type."],null],["cw35",0,3,"What is the correct order of frames when a client joins a WPA2-Personal BSS?",["Authentication, probe, 4-way handshake, association","Probe, association, 4-way handshake, authentication","Probe, authentication, association, 4-way handshake","4-way handshake, probe, authentication, association"],2,"The client discovers the BSS, performs open system authentication, associates, and then runs the 4-way handshake to derive keys.","Domain 3 – Joining a BSS",["The client cannot authenticate before discovering the BSS, and association must come before the 4-way handshake.","Open system authentication happens before association, not after the 4-way handshake.",null,"The 4-way handshake needs an association in place first, so it cannot come before probing."],null],["cw36",0,3,"A capture shows a laptop sending probe requests on each channel in turn. What is the laptop doing?",["Active scanning","Passive scanning","Reassociating","Deauthenticating"],0,"Active scanning means sending probe requests and waiting for probe responses. Passive scanning only listens for beacons.","Domain 3 – Joining a BSS",[null,"Passive scanning means listening for beacons without transmitting; sending probe requests is active scanning.","Reassociation is a request to a specific AP when roaming within an ESS, not probes sent on each channel in turn.","Deauthentication ends an existing connection and is not sent on each channel to discover networks."],null],["cw37",0,3,"A station hears a frame whose Duration field is 300 µs and holds off transmitting for that time. Which mechanism is this?",["Energy detect CCA","Virtual carrier sense (NAV)","The random backoff timer","The SIFS interval"],1,"The NAV timer is set from the Duration field of frames heard, so the station defers even if it later cannot sense energy. CCA is physical carrier sense.","Domain 3 – Medium access",["Energy detect CCA is physical carrier sense that reacts to energy currently on the channel, not a timer set from a Duration field.",null,"The random backoff timer is drawn from the contention window after the medium goes idle; it is not set by another station's Duration field.","SIFS is a fixed, very short gap before responses such as ACKs, not a 300 µs deferral taken from a heard frame."],null],["cw38",0,3,"What does an RTS/CTS exchange achieve before a data frame is sent?",["It checks the client's power save state","It encrypts the next data frame","It asks the AP for a higher data rate","It reserves the medium through the NAV"],3,"The CTS from the AP sets the NAV of every station that hears it, including hidden stations that did not hear the RTS, so they defer.","Domain 3 – Protection",["Power save state is signaled with the power management bit, PS-Poll or null frames, not RTS/CTS.","RTS/CTS carry no keys and do not encrypt anything; encryption uses keys from the 4-way handshake.","Rate selection is handled by the transmitter's rate adaptation algorithm; RTS/CTS does not negotiate data rates.",null],null],["cw39",0,3,"Which interframe space is the shortest and is used before an ACK?",["SIFS","DIFS","AIFS","EIFS"],0,"SIFS is the shortest gap, giving ACKs and CTS frames priority. DIFS and AIFS are longer and used for contention; EIFS follows a corrupted frame.","Domain 3 – Medium access",[null,"DIFS is longer than SIFS and is used by stations contending for the medium under DCF, not before an ACK.","AIFS varies by WMM access category and is used for contention, so it is longer than SIFS.","EIFS is an extended wait used after receiving a corrupted frame, making it the longest of these spaces."],null],["cw40",0,3,"With WMM enabled, voice frames reach the air sooner than best-effort frames. Why?",["Higher transmit power for voice","A dedicated voice-only channel","Shorter AIFS and smaller contention windows","A guaranteed slot in each beacon"],2,"EDCA gives the voice access category a shorter AIFS and smaller contention window, so it usually wins contention. WMM does not reserve channels or slots.","Domain 3 – QoS",["WMM does not change transmit power by access category; priority comes from contention timing.","WMM does not reserve a separate channel for voice; all access categories share the same channel.",null,"EDCA is contention-based; beacons do not reserve guaranteed transmit slots for voice frames."],null],["cw41",0,3,"A dozing phone wakes for a beacon and learns the AP is holding unicast frames for it. Which beacon field told it?",["The DS parameter set","The SSID element","The Traffic Indication Map","The supported rates element"],2,"The TIM lists association IDs with buffered unicast frames. The DS parameter set gives the channel and the SSID element names the network.","Domain 3 – Power management",["The DS parameter set element tells stations the AP's current channel, not whether frames are buffered.","The SSID element carries the network name and has nothing to do with buffered frames.",null,"The supported rates element lists the data rates the BSS supports, not buffered traffic."],null],["cw42",0,3,"Push-to-talk badges on a WLAN have long delays for multicast voice after the admin raised the DTIM interval to save battery. What explains this?",["Group frames wait for the next DTIM beacon","The TIM stops listing unicast frames","The AP drops multicast from sleeping clients","A higher DTIM forces a lower data rate"],0,"The AP buffers broadcast and multicast until a DTIM beacon, so a larger DTIM interval saves power but adds delay to group traffic.","Domain 3 – Power management",[null,"The TIM continues to list clients with buffered unicast frames in every beacon regardless of the DTIM interval.","The AP buffers group traffic for dozing clients and releases it after a DTIM beacon; it does not simply drop it.","The DTIM interval controls when buffered group frames are released, not the data rate they are sent at."],null],["cw43",0,3,"In an 802.11 WLAN, which device makes the final decision to roam?",["The RADIUS server","The access point","The WLAN controller","The client station"],3,"Roaming is client-driven. 802.11v lets the network suggest a better AP, but the client chooses whether and when to move.","Domain 3 – Roaming",["The RADIUS server authenticates users and has no role in choosing which AP a client joins.","An AP can suggest a move with 802.11v or disconnect a client, but the client decides whether and where to roam.","A controller can influence roaming with steering and 802.11v hints, but the station makes the final decision.",null],null],["cw44",0,3,"Which identifier names a single basic service set and is normally the MAC address of an AP radio?",["SSID","BSSID","ESSID","OUI"],1,"The BSSID is a 48-bit MAC-style address for one BSS. The SSID is the human-readable network name that can span many BSSs.","Domain 3 – Addressing",["The SSID is the human-readable network name, which can be shared by many BSSs, not a MAC-style identifier for one BSS.",null,"The ESSID is the network name used across an extended service set of many APs, not a single BSS.","An OUI is the first 24 bits of a MAC address that identifies the manufacturer, not a whole BSS."],null],["cw45",0,3,"In a controller-based WLAN, which protocol do lightweight APs commonly use to talk to the controller?",["BGP","LLDP","STP","CAPWAP"],3,"CAPWAP is the standard tunneling protocol for AP-to-controller control and optional data traffic. LLDP only advertises neighbor information.","Domain 3 – Devices",["BGP is an internet routing protocol between autonomous systems; it is not used between APs and a WLAN controller.","LLDP advertises device identity and capabilities to directly connected neighbors; it does not tunnel AP control traffic.","STP prevents Layer 2 loops on switched networks and has no role in AP-to-controller communication.",null],null],["cw46",0,3,"A new AP needs about 25 W at the device, and the switch supports only 802.3af. What is the minimum PoE standard the switch should support?",["802.3af (PoE)","802.3at (PoE+)","802.3ab","802.3u"],1,"802.3af provides at most 12.95 W at the device; 802.3at provides up to 25.5 W. 802.3ab and 802.3u are Ethernet speed standards, not PoE.","Domain 3 – PoE",["802.3af guarantees only 12.95 W at the device, which is well short of the 25 W this AP needs.",null,"802.3ab is the 1000BASE-T Gigabit Ethernet standard over copper and does not deliver power.","802.3u is Fast Ethernet (100 Mbps) and defines no power delivery."],null],["cw47",0,3,"A small shop has two APs, no controller and no cloud account. Each AP holds its own configuration. What kind of APs are these?",["Cloud-managed APs","Lightweight APs","Autonomous APs","Mesh portal APs"],2,"Autonomous APs contain the management, control and data planes and are configured individually. Lightweight APs need a controller.","Domain 3 – Devices",["Cloud-managed APs are configured from a cloud service, and this shop has no cloud account.","Lightweight APs depend on a controller for configuration and control, and there is no controller here.",null,"A mesh portal AP bridges wireless mesh traffic to the wired network; nothing here describes a mesh."],null],["cw48",0,3,"A parking garage has no cabling in its upper levels. The APs there must forward traffic wirelessly to an AP that has a wired uplink. Which design is this?",["Mesh","Band steering","Channel bonding","Load balancing"],0,"In a mesh, APs use wireless backhaul to reach a root or portal AP connected to the wired network. The others are radio features, not backhaul designs.","Domain 3 – Devices",[null,"Band steering nudges dual-band clients toward 5 GHz; it does not provide wireless backhaul between APs.","Channel bonding combines adjacent channels for more throughput; it does not link APs wirelessly to a wired uplink.","Load balancing spreads clients across APs; it does not carry traffic from unwired APs to a wired one."],null],["cw49",0,3,"In a capture, which frame control flag shows that a frame is a retransmission?",["More Data","Retry","Power Management","Protected Frame"],1,"The Retry bit is set when a frame is sent again. More Data tells a dozing client more frames are buffered.","Domain 3 – Frames",["More Data tells a dozing client that the AP has more buffered frames for it; it does not mark retransmissions.",null,"The Power Management bit tells the AP whether the station is going to doze, not whether the frame is a retry.","The Protected Frame bit shows that the frame body is encrypted, not that it was resent."],null],["cw50",0,3,"802.11n and later radios send several MPDUs in one A-MPDU. How does the receiver confirm them efficiently?",["A bit in the next beacon","A separate ACK for each MPDU","A CTS-to-self frame","A single Block Ack frame"],3,"Block Ack confirms many frames at once with a bitmap, keeping aggregation efficient. CTS-to-self is a protection mechanism.","Domain 3 – Aggregation",["Beacons do not acknowledge data frames; they announce the BSS and buffered traffic.","Sending a separate ACK for every MPDU would waste the airtime that aggregation is meant to save.","CTS-to-self is a protection mechanism that reserves the medium before transmitting; it does not acknowledge received frames.",null],null],["cw51",0,4,"An auditorium must serve 800 users. Which approach best fits a capacity-based design?",["More APs at lower power on 20 MHz channels","Fewer APs at maximum power for full coverage","80 MHz channels on every AP in 2.4 GHz","One high-gain omni in the center of the room"],0,"Capacity design makes smaller cells so each AP serves fewer clients and channels can be reused. Maximum power and wide channels reduce reuse.","Domain 4 – Capacity design",[null,"Fewer APs at maximum power creates large cells with many clients per AP, which suits coverage, not 800 users.","80 MHz channels do not fit in the 83.5 MHz 2.4 GHz band, and wide channels reduce reuse in a dense room anyway.","A single omni puts every user on one AP and one channel, which cannot provide the airtime 800 users need."],null],["cw52",0,4,"A hospital is designing for Wi-Fi phones. Which target is common vendor guidance?",["About −30 dBm with no SNR target","About −85 dBm with an SNR near 5 dB","About −67 dBm with an SNR near 25 dB","About −90 dBm with an SNR near 10 dB"],2,"Many voice client vendors recommend around −67 dBm and 25 dB SNR so higher rates and low retries are possible. −85 dBm is near the edge of usable signal.","Domain 4 – Design targets",["-30 dBm is an extremely strong signal found only right next to an AP, and ignoring SNR ignores noise-related failures.","-85 dBm with 5 dB SNR is near the edge of receive sensitivity and would give low rates, retries and dropped calls.",null,"-90 dBm is close to the noise floor and a 10 dB SNR supports only the lowest rates, far too poor for voice."],null],["cw53",0,4,"Why do voice designs call for each area to hear a second AP at a usable signal level?",["So the controller can load balance by SSID","So each phone can join two APs at once","So phones can roam without dropping calls","So radar detection is shared across APs"],2,"Secondary coverage gives the phone a roaming target before the current signal becomes too weak, avoiding gaps and dropped calls.","Domain 4 – Design targets",["Load balancing is not tied to SSIDs, and secondary coverage exists to give phones a roaming target, not to spread SSIDs.","An 802.11 station associates with only one AP at a time; secondary coverage does not let it join two.",null,"Radar detection is a DFS function each radio performs itself; it is not the reason for overlapping voice coverage."],null],["cw54",0,4,"Which architecture keeps configuration and monitoring in a vendor-hosted service while user traffic is forwarded locally by the APs?",["Cloud-managed","Autonomous","Centralized controller","Standalone mesh"],0,"Cloud-managed WLANs host the management plane in the cloud; data stays local. Autonomous APs have no central management at all.","Domain 4 – Architectures",[null,"Autonomous APs have no central management service; each is configured on its own.","A centralized controller hosts management on premises and often tunnels user traffic to itself, rather than a vendor-hosted service.","A standalone mesh describes wireless backhaul between APs, not where management is hosted."],null],["cw55",0,4,"Branch APs tunnel all user traffic across the WAN to a controller at headquarters, and branch users complain about slow access to a local printer. What is the best change?",["Move the branch APs to 40 MHz channels","Raise AP power at the branch office","Add another SSID for printer access","Forward branch traffic locally at the AP"],3,"Local (distributed) forwarding keeps branch traffic on the branch LAN instead of hairpinning across the WAN. Radio changes do not fix the traffic path.","Domain 4 – Data forwarding",["Wider channels change radio capacity but not the traffic path; frames would still hairpin through headquarters.","Higher AP power affects coverage, not the WAN round trip caused by tunneling traffic to a distant controller.","A new SSID would still be tunneled to headquarters unless its forwarding mode changes, and it adds beacon overhead.",null],null],["cw56",0,4,"Radio resource management that adjusts channels and power across APs belongs to which plane?",["Management plane","Control plane","Data plane","User plane"],1,"The control plane coordinates the network, such as RRM and roaming. The management plane handles configuration and monitoring; the data plane forwards user traffic.","Domain 4 – Planes",["The management plane handles configuration, firmware and monitoring, not live coordination such as RRM.",null,"The data plane forwards user frames; it does not decide channel and power settings.","The user plane is another name for the data plane that carries user traffic, not the coordination functions."],null],["cw57",0,4,"An autonomous AP will serve three SSIDs, each mapped to a different VLAN. How should the switch port for the AP be configured?",["As a routed port with no VLANs","As an access port in the guest VLAN","As an access port in the native VLAN","As an 802.1Q trunk with those VLANs"],3,"Carrying several VLANs to the AP requires an 802.1Q trunk. An access port carries only one VLAN.","Domain 4 – SSIDs and VLANs",["A routed port has no VLANs, so it cannot deliver three separate VLANs to the AP.","An access port in the guest VLAN would carry only guest traffic, leaving the other two SSIDs without their VLANs.","An access port carries only one untagged VLAN, so the SSIDs could not be mapped to different VLANs.",null],null],["cw58",0,4,"A site has eight SSIDs on every AP and high channel utilization even when few users are active. What is the likely cause?",["DFS events in the 2.4 GHz band","Beacon and probe overhead from many SSIDs","Too few spatial streams on the APs","Overly strong WPA3 encryption"],1,"Each SSID beacons separately, usually at a low basic rate, so many SSIDs consume airtime before any data flows. DFS applies to 5 GHz only.","Domain 4 – SSID design",["DFS applies to certain 5 GHz channels only; there is no DFS in 2.4 GHz.",null,"Fewer spatial streams limit peak throughput but do not create high utilization when few users are active.","Encryption strength does not add airtime; WPA3 frames are about the same size as WPA2 frames."],null],["cw59",0,4,"An admin disables the 1, 2, 5.5 and 11 Mbps rates on 2.4 GHz. What is the main benefit?",["Each AP cell becomes larger","Legacy 802.11b clients connect more easily","Frames use less airtime, which helps roaming","Beacons are no longer transmitted"],2,"Higher minimum rates shorten management frames and make cells effectively smaller, encouraging clients to roam. It locks out 802.11b clients rather than helping them.","Domain 4 – Data rates",["Raising the minimum data rate makes cells effectively smaller, not larger.","Disabling 1-11 Mbps prevents 802.11b-only clients from connecting at all.",null,"Beacons are still sent, just at a higher basic rate, so they use less airtime."],null],["cw60",0,4,"Which feature encourages dual-band clients to connect on 5 GHz instead of 2.4 GHz?",["Band steering","Load balancing","BSS coloring","Dynamic Frequency Selection"],0,"Band steering delays or ignores 2.4 GHz probe responses for dual-band clients so they join on 5 GHz. Load balancing spreads clients across APs.","Domain 4 – Client steering",[null,"Load balancing spreads clients across APs by load; it does not specifically push clients to the 5 GHz band.","BSS coloring improves spatial reuse between overlapping 802.11ax BSSs; it does not steer clients between bands.","DFS is a radar avoidance requirement on some 5 GHz channels; it does not move clients from 2.4 GHz."],null],["cw61",0,4,"A warehouse uses 1x1 scanners that support only 2.4 GHz. Why should this be found before the design is done?",["Scanners can use only DFS channels in 5 GHz","The design must suit the weakest critical client","2.4 GHz clients need 160 MHz channels","It only affects the choice of SSID name"],1,"Coverage, band and rate choices must work for the least capable devices the business depends on, so a 5 GHz-only design would fail these scanners.","Domain 4 – Requirements",["Scanners limited to 2.4 GHz cannot use 5 GHz channels at all, DFS or not.",null,"160 MHz channels do not exist in 2.4 GHz, and 1x1 scanners certainly do not need them.","Client band support drives AP placement, band and rate decisions, not just the SSID name."],null],["cw62",0,4,"A switch has a 370 W PoE budget. The plan adds 16 APs that can each draw 25.5 W. What is the concern?",["PoE budget applies only to phones","The APs need 802.3af instead","The budget suits 32 of these APs","The 408 W demand exceeds the budget"],3,"16 × 25.5 W = 408 W, above the 370 W budget, so some APs may not power up or may run in a reduced mode.","Domain 4 – PoE planning",["PoE budgets cover every powered device on the switch, including APs, not just phones.","802.3af supplies only 12.95 W at the device, far below the 25.5 W these APs can draw.","370 W divided by 25.5 W supports only about 14 of these APs at full draw, not 32.",null],null],["cw63",0,4,"Several nearby 5 GHz APs are all on the same 80 MHz channel and users see slow throughput. What design change helps most?",["Use narrower channels to allow more reuse","Increase transmit power on every AP","Add more SSIDs to split the users","Move every AP to channel 36 at 160 MHz"],0,"Narrower channels give more non-overlapping channels, reducing co-channel contention. More power or wider channels increase contention.","Domain 4 – Channel reuse",[null,"Raising power makes APs hear each other even more, increasing co-channel contention instead of reducing it.","Extra SSIDs share the same channel and add beacon overhead, so they reduce airtime rather than splitting load.","Putting every AP on one 160 MHz channel leaves no reuse at all and makes contention worse."],null],["cw64",0,5,"A small business worries that someone could capture its Wi-Fi handshake and guess the password offline. Which change helps most?",["Enable MAC address filtering","Hide the SSID from beacon frames","Move to WPA3-Personal using SAE","Switch from CCMP to TKIP"],2,"SAE resists offline dictionary attacks against a captured exchange. Hiding the SSID and MAC filtering are easily bypassed, and TKIP is deprecated.","Domain 5 – WPA3",["MAC filtering does not protect the handshake, and MAC addresses are sent in the clear and easy to spoof.","A hidden SSID still appears in probe and association frames and does nothing to stop offline passphrase guessing.",null,"TKIP is deprecated and weaker than CCMP, and it still uses the same PSK handshake that can be attacked offline."],null],["cw65",0,5,"A café wants an open network with no password but encrypted traffic for each guest. Which option fits?",["WEP with a shared key","WPA2-Personal with a posted key","Enhanced Open (OWE)","A hidden SSID"],2,"OWE gives each client unique encryption without a passphrase. A shared posted key lets anyone with it attack others' sessions.","Domain 5 – Enhanced Open",["WEP is broken and requires a shared key, so it is neither password-free nor meaningfully secure.","A posted WPA2 key lets anyone who knows it derive other guests' session keys from captured handshakes, and it is not password-free.",null,"Hiding the SSID provides no encryption at all and the name still leaks in probe frames."],null],["cw66",0,5,"In a WPA2-Enterprise network, which 802.1X role does the access point or controller play?",["Authenticator","Supplicant","Authentication server","Certificate authority"],0,"The client is the supplicant, the AP or controller is the authenticator, and RADIUS is the authentication server.","Domain 5 – 802.1X",[null,"The supplicant is the client device requesting access, not the AP or controller.","The authentication server is usually RADIUS, which checks credentials behind the AP.","A certificate authority issues certificates; it is not one of the three 802.1X roles on the WLAN."],null],["cw67",0,5,"A company wants mutual certificate authentication with no user passwords sent over Wi-Fi. Which EAP method fits?",["LEAP","PEAP-MSCHAPv2","EAP-TTLS with PAP","EAP-TLS"],3,"EAP-TLS requires certificates on both server and client. PEAP and TTLS normally use passwords inside a tunnel; LEAP is obsolete.","Domain 5 – EAP",["LEAP is an obsolete Cisco method that uses passwords (MS-CHAP) and is vulnerable to dictionary attacks.","PEAP-MSCHAPv2 uses only a server certificate and authenticates the user with a password inside the tunnel.","EAP-TTLS with PAP sends a user password inside a TLS tunnel and does not require client certificates.",null],null],["cw68",0,5,"Which encryption protocol is mandatory for WPA2?",["TKIP, based on RC4","CCMP, based on AES","WEP with 104-bit keys","Triple DES in CBC mode"],1,"WPA2 certification requires CCMP (AES). TKIP was an interim fix for WEP hardware and is now deprecated.","Domain 5 – Encryption",["TKIP was the WPA stopgap built on RC4 for older hardware; it is optional in WPA2 and now deprecated.",null,"WEP predates WPA2, is broken, and is not allowed in WPA2 at all.","Triple DES is not used by any 802.11 security suite; WPA2 is based on AES."],null],["cw69",0,5,"Clients keep dropping off an SSID, and a capture shows floods of deauthentication frames spoofing the AP's MAC address. Which feature prevents this attack?",["Band steering","Fast BSS transition (802.11r)","Neighbor reports (802.11k)","Protected Management Frames (802.11w)"],3,"802.11w protects deauthentication and disassociation frames so forged ones are ignored. 802.11r only speeds up roaming.","Domain 5 – PMF",["Band steering moves dual-band clients to 5 GHz and gives no protection against forged management frames.","802.11r speeds up roaming with pre-derived keys but does not authenticate deauthentication frames.","802.11k neighbor reports help clients choose roaming targets and do nothing against spoofed deauthentication.",null],null],["cw70",0,5,"WIPS finds an unmanaged AP plugged into a conference room wall jack on the corporate LAN. How is it classified?",["Neighbor AP","Rogue AP","Mesh portal","Managed AP"],1,"A rogue AP is unauthorized and connected to the organization's wired network. A neighbor AP belongs to someone else and is not on your LAN.","Domain 5 – Rogues and WIPS",["A neighbor AP belongs to someone else and is not connected to your wired LAN, unlike this one.",null,"A mesh portal is an authorized AP that bridges mesh traffic to the wired network, not an unmanaged device.","A managed AP is authorized and controlled by your WLAN system, which this unmanaged AP is not."],null],["cw71",0,5,"In WPA2-Personal, where does the Pairwise Master Key come from?",["The group temporal key","The RADIUS server's reply","The passphrase and SSID","The AP's BSSID alone"],2,"With a PSK, the PMK is derived from the passphrase and SSID. In Enterprise mode it comes from the EAP exchange with RADIUS.","Domain 5 – Key hierarchy",["The GTK is delivered during the 4-way handshake and is derived separately; it is not the source of the PMK.","RADIUS provides the PMK source in Enterprise mode only; WPA2-Personal uses no RADIUS server.",null,"The BSSID (AP MAC address) is used in deriving the PTK, but the PMK comes from the passphrase and SSID."],null],["cw72",0,5,"A guest SSID must stop guest devices from reaching one another. Which setting should be used?",["Client isolation","MAC filtering","SSID hiding","A longer DTIM interval"],0,"Client (peer-to-peer) isolation blocks traffic between wireless clients. MAC filtering and hiding do not stop client-to-client traffic.","Domain 5 – Guest access",[null,"MAC filtering only controls which devices may associate; it does not stop associated guests from talking to each other.","Hiding the SSID only removes the name from beacons and has no effect on traffic between clients.","The DTIM interval controls delivery of buffered group frames to dozing clients, not client-to-client traffic."],null],["cw73",0,6,"An engineer walks a site recording signal and noise from every AP without associating to any of them. Which survey is this?",["Active survey","Passive survey","Predictive survey","Spectrum-only survey"],1,"A passive survey listens to all APs. An active survey associates and measures throughput, retries and roaming.","Domain 6 – Survey types",["An active survey associates to the network to measure throughput, retries and roaming, which this engineer is not doing.",null,"A predictive survey is done in software from floor plans, without walking the site with a live adapter.","A spectrum-only survey records raw RF energy, not per-AP signal and noise from decoded Wi-Fi frames."],null],["cw74",0,6,"Before a new building is finished, a designer uses floor plans and wall attenuation values in software to plan AP locations. Which survey is this?",["Validation survey","Passive survey","Active survey","Predictive survey"],3,"A predictive survey models RF with software and building data before APs are installed. The others need measurements on site.","Domain 6 – Survey types",["A validation survey measures a live network after installation; here the building is not even finished.","A passive survey requires walking the site and hearing real APs, which is impossible before installation.","An active survey requires associating to a working network on site, which does not exist yet.",null],null],["cw75",0,6,"Users in a break room lose 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi around lunchtime, but 5 GHz is fine. Which tool best identifies the source?",["Spectrum analyzer","Protocol analyzer","Cable tester","RADIUS debug log"],0,"The likely cause is non-Wi-Fi energy such as a microwave oven, which only a spectrum analyzer shows. A protocol analyzer only decodes 802.11 frames.","Domain 6 – Spectrum analysis",[null,"A protocol analyzer decodes 802.11 frames only, so it can show retries but not a non-Wi-Fi source such as a microwave oven.","A cable tester checks wired Ethernet cabling and cannot detect RF interference.","RADIUS debug logs show authentication results, which would not explain a 2.4 GHz-only lunchtime outage."],null],["cw76",0,6,"A laptop running Wireshark shows only its own traffic and no beacons from other networks. What must change to see all 802.11 frames on a channel?",["Join every SSID to be captured","Turn on promiscuous mode on the switch port","Put the adapter in monitor mode on that channel","Use a wired mirror port on the AP uplink"],2,"Monitor mode lets a wireless adapter capture all 802.11 frames on its channel, including management and control frames. Wired mirroring never shows RF frames.","Domain 6 – Protocol analysis",["Joining SSIDs does not let the adapter see other stations' frames; it still captures only its own traffic.","Promiscuous mode on a switch port is a wired concept and does not affect what a wireless adapter hears.",null,"A wired mirror port shows Ethernet frames after the AP converts them, never beacons or other 802.11 RF frames."],null],["cw77",0,6,"Two laptops at opposite ends of a long room both connect to the central AP but cannot hear each other, and retries are high. What is the likely problem?",["Co-channel contention","Adjacent channel interference","Hidden node","Sticky client"],2,"Stations that cannot hear each other transmit at the same time and collide at the AP. RTS/CTS, AP placement or smaller cells can help.","Domain 6 – Troubleshooting",["Co-channel contention involves devices that hear each other and share airtime; these laptops cannot hear each other.","Adjacent channel interference comes from overlapping neighboring channels; both laptops here use the same AP and channel.",null,"A sticky client stays on a distant AP instead of roaming; these laptops are both connected to the central AP correctly."],null],["cw78",0,6,"Twelve APs in an open office are all on channel 6 at full power, and airtime is saturated. What is the best fix?",["Reduce power and spread APs across channels","Raise power on all APs to beat noise","Enable 40 MHz channels in 2.4 GHz","Add a second SSID on channel 6"],0,"The APs hear each other and must share airtime (co-channel contention). Lower power and more channels, ideally 5 GHz, restore capacity.","Domain 6 – Troubleshooting",[null,"Raising power makes the APs hear each other farther away, increasing co-channel contention rather than fixing it.","40 MHz in 2.4 GHz leaves only one clean channel, which would make contention worse.","A second SSID on the same channel adds beacon overhead and shares the same saturated airtime."],null],["cw79",0,6,"A client stays connected to a distant AP with a weak signal even when standing under another AP. What is this called?",["An evil twin","A hidden node","A rogue client","A sticky client"],3,"Sticky clients delay roaming. 802.11k/v, raising minimum basic rates and tuning power can encourage them to move.","Domain 6 – Troubleshooting",["An evil twin is a malicious AP imitating a legitimate SSID; this is a normal client failing to roam.","A hidden node is a station that other stations cannot hear, causing collisions, not one that refuses to roam.","A rogue client is an unauthorized device; this client is authorized but slow to roam.",null],null],["cw80",0,6,"A phone shows full signal bars but uploads are very slow, and the AP is set to its maximum power. What is a likely cause?",["The phone uses too high a DTIM value","The AP's power far exceeds the phone's","The AP's 2.4 GHz band is turned off","The SSID is sent in every beacon"],1,"The phone hears the strong AP, but its weaker transmissions reach the AP poorly, causing uplink retries. Matching AP power to client capability helps.","Domain 6 – Troubleshooting",["DTIM affects how often a dozing client wakes for group traffic, not uplink throughput.",null,"Turning off 2.4 GHz would not make a connected phone's uploads slow while it shows full bars.","Sending the SSID in beacons is normal behavior and has no effect on the phone's uplink."],null],["cw81",0,6,"A capture made next to the AP looks clean, but the user far away still complains. Where should the next capture be made?",["Next to a different AP","At the core switch","On the RADIUS server","Near the affected client"],3,"Capture close to the client on its channel to see what it hears and sends. Wired captures miss RF behavior entirely.","Domain 6 – Protocol analysis",["Another AP's location shows what that AP hears, not what the affected client is experiencing.","A core switch capture shows wired frames only and misses RF problems such as retries or interference near the client.","The RADIUS server shows authentication results, not the RF conditions the client experiences.",null],null],["cw82",0,6,"Which capture statistic most directly points to RF problems such as interference or collisions?",["A high beacon count","A high retry rate","A long DTIM interval","Many probe responses"],1,"Retransmissions mean frames were not acknowledged, often from interference, collisions or weak signal. Beacon counts are normal background traffic.","Domain 6 – Metrics",["Beacons are normal background management traffic, so a high count does not indicate interference or collisions.",null,"The DTIM interval is a configuration setting for group traffic delivery, not a symptom of RF problems.","Many probe responses usually reflect clients scanning or many SSIDs, not frame loss from interference."],null],["cw83",0,6,"After APs are installed, the customer wants proof that the design targets were met. Which activity provides this?",["A wired cable certification","A predictive design refresh","A post-installation validation survey","A new requirements interview"],2,"A validation survey measures the live network against the agreed requirements. Predictive models are estimates, not proof.","Domain 6 – Validation",["Cable certification proves the wired runs meet their category spec, not that RF design targets are met.","A predictive refresh is still a software model and cannot prove what the installed network actually delivers.",null,"A requirements interview gathers goals before design; it does not measure whether they were achieved."],null],["cw84",0,6,"All WPA2-Enterprise clients began failing authentication on the same morning with no configuration changes. PEAP logs show a server certificate error. What is the likely cause?",["The RADIUS server certificate expired","The PSK was changed overnight","The AP moved to a DFS channel","The DHCP scope ran out of leases"],0,"An expired server certificate breaks PEAP and EAP-TLS for every client at once. There is no PSK in Enterprise mode, and DHCP happens after authentication.","Domain 6 – Troubleshooting",[null,"WPA2-Enterprise uses 802.1X/EAP and has no PSK, so changing one could not cause this.","A DFS channel change causes a brief disconnect, not PEAP server certificate errors for every client.","DHCP happens after successful authentication, so an exhausted scope would not cause certificate errors in PEAP."],null],["cw85",0,6,"On a WPA2-Personal network, a capture shows EAPOL messages 1 and 2 but the AP never sends message 3. What is most likely wrong?",["The client's RADIUS certificate expired","The client has the wrong passphrase","The AP lacks a DHCP reservation","The AP uses a hidden SSID"],1,"The AP checks the MIC in message 2; with a mismatched passphrase it fails and the AP does not continue. Personal mode uses no RADIUS.","Domain 6 – Troubleshooting",["WPA2-Personal uses a pre-shared key, not RADIUS, so no RADIUS certificate is involved.",null,"DHCP runs only after the 4-way handshake succeeds, so it cannot stop message 3 from being sent.","A hidden SSID affects discovery only; the client has already associated and started the handshake."],null],["cw86",0,6,"Two neighboring 2.4 GHz APs are on channels 1 and 3. What problem does this create?",["DFS radar events","Co-channel contention only","Hidden node collisions","Adjacent channel interference"],3,"Overlapping but different channels cannot share the medium properly and corrupt each other's frames. Use 1, 6 and 11 instead.","Domain 6 – Troubleshooting",["DFS applies to certain 5 GHz channels, not to 2.4 GHz channels 1 and 3.","Co-channel contention happens when APs share the same channel; channels 1 and 3 are different but overlapping.","A hidden node is a station that others cannot hear; this problem comes from overlapping channel assignments.",null],null],["cw87",0,6,"During an active survey, the engineer places a temporary AP on a stand at planned locations to find the cell edge. What is this method called?",["AP-on-a-stick survey","Predictive modeling","Passive walk-through","Spectrum sweep"],0,"AP-on-a-stick uses a temporary AP to measure real coverage at candidate spots before final mounting.","Domain 6 – Survey types",[null,"Predictive modeling is done in software from floor plans, without placing a real AP on site.","A passive walk-through listens to already installed APs rather than placing a temporary AP at candidate spots.","A spectrum sweep measures RF energy and interference, not the coverage cell edge of a temporary AP."],null],["cw88",0,6,"A spectrum analyzer shows a source using nearly 100% duty cycle across channels 1 to 4. What happens to Wi-Fi devices there?",["They raise power until the noise stops","They switch to 6 GHz on their own","They defer constantly and cannot send","They ignore it because it is not 802.11"],2,"A constant non-Wi-Fi signal keeps CCA busy or ruins SNR, so devices defer or fail to decode. Remove the source or move channels.","Domain 6 – Spectrum analysis",["Wi-Fi devices cannot out-shout a constant interferer, and raising power does not clear energy another device is producing.","Clients do not move bands on their own because of interference; 6 GHz also requires 6 GHz-capable hardware.",null,"CCA energy detect reacts to any strong energy, not only 802.11 frames, so non-Wi-Fi signals still block transmissions."],null],["cw89",0,6,"A laptop associates to the corporate SSID and completes the handshake, but it gets a 169.254.x.x address. Where should troubleshooting focus next?",["The 802.11 authentication frames","The antenna's polarization","DHCP and VLAN mapping for the SSID","The AP's channel and power"],2,"The Wi-Fi link works; the problem is Layer 3 addressing, often a wrong VLAN on the SSID or trunk, or a DHCP scope or relay issue.","Domain 6 – Troubleshooting",["The 802.11 authentication and 4-way handshake already succeeded, so the link layer is working.","Polarization affects signal strength, but the laptop already has a working Wi-Fi link.",null,"Channel and power are RF settings; the laptop is connected and the failure is IP addressing."],null],["cw90",0,6,"Which channel utilization finding most suggests a cell cannot take more clients?",["Utilization stays high while busy","Utilization is low all day","Utilization rises for a moment","Utilization is the same on each band"],0,"Consistently high utilization during busy periods means little airtime is left for new clients. Brief spikes are normal.","Domain 6 – Metrics",[null,"Low utilization all day means plenty of airtime is free, so the cell can take more clients.","Brief spikes are normal as traffic bursts and do not show that the cell is out of capacity.","Equal utilization on each band says nothing about whether that utilization is high or low."],null]]);
